35:Summary: 'For a new intern: the parts of the Typed to RV32 compiler that decide how many instructions a program executes, the measured gap to the reference compiler and its causes, and the design of the four changes that close it: an inlining pass, loop rotation with a block layout, copy coalescing and constant homes in the backend, behind a new O3 pipeline and default-off backend options.'
38:WhenToUse: Before changing src/typed/inline.ts, src/typed/rotate.ts, src/typed/backend/{coalesce,homes,layout,estimate}.ts, or the O3 pipeline.
70:The reference compiler does three things ours does not: it inlines calls, it rotates loops so that the loop test sits at the bottom, and it unrolls kernels. The analysis behind the Candela numbers (IRWB-001, f32 backend diary steps 8–10) found three causes on our side: calls that `kernel` inlines, f32 constants rebuilt at every use, and register copies that the allocator does not remove. This milestone addresses four items of the IRWB-002 research agenda:
73:- **G1, loop rotation and block layout**: make a loop iteration end in one conditional branch instead of a jump and a branch.
74:- **H2, copy coalescing**: give the two sides of a register copy the same register when that is safe, so the copy disappears.
101:Two of the four changes act in the middle of this path, on `typed.blocks`: inlining and loop rotation are passes. The other two act inside the backend: coalescing is part of allocation, and constants in registers are part of selection. A fifth piece, the block layout, is a backend option that decides the order in which a function's blocks are emitted. The rest of Part 1 explains each stage at the level of detail these changes need.
176:`from` is the entity the new one was made from, `via` the pass, `rev` the revision it was made in, and `because` the entities or facts that make the change valid. `Program.why(id)` (`program.ts:457`) follows `from` and `because` recursively through all stored revisions. That is how the workbench answers "why is this statement here?": for Blend's hoisted `s:blend.15 inv = ((a * b) + 64)`, `why` reaches source line 5 through `from`, and the loop fact through `because` (IRWB-002 guide §11). Inlining must keep this working: an inlined statement must lead `why` both to the callee statement it copies and to the call it replaced.
208:Inlining interacts with both. Without entries, an inlined callee stays in the program and is still compared on its own cases; its body is also compared, inside its callers, through the callers' cases. With entries, `dfe` can remove a callee that no longer has a call site, and it is then checked only through the entries.
228:**Hints** let a parameter prefer its argument register and a returned value `a0` (`select.ts:724–725`, `:799`; `regalloc.ts:294–298`). They are the only form of coalescing today, and they apply only to copies from or to a physical register.
234:- **A copy whose two sides got the same physical register is dropped** ("coalesced", `compile.ts:158–162`). This is why coalescing can be done by giving two virtual registers the same register: the copy then disappears without further work.
290:- **The `jal` back to the test.** If the test sat at the bottom of the loop, the iteration would end with one conditional branch back to the body. This is loop rotation plus a block order in which the exit follows the loop (sections 10 and 12). Estimate: 40 instructions.
299:**Insertion sort** gains nothing from O2: 2,022 instructions at r0 and after O2. Its inner loop `while (j > 0 && work.charCodeAt(j - 1) > work.charCodeAt(j))` is lifted into a test block `bb:sort.4` (`if (j > 0) goto .5 else .6`), the `&&` is a temporary `t11`, and the body reads `j` through three copies (`t5 = j`, `t6 = j`, `t8 = j`) that sit in different blocks, so neither `lvn` nor `subst` removes them. Per inner iteration, the image executes the `jal` back to `bb:sort.4` and three copies that coalescing can remove (`various/06-…`, the sort listing).
303:**Candela** differs in what dominates. Our O2 image executes 4,043,187 more instructions than `kernel` over the decided cases (the net gap; 28,706,118 against 24,662,932). The functions where ours is slower account for 5,307,267 of them, and the ones where ours is faster win back 1,264,080 (`scripts/03-candela-gap-totals.mjs` on IRWB-001's `f32-backend-01-candela-images.tsv`, output in `various/07-candela-o2-gap-totals.txt`). Two functions hold half of the 5.31 million: `fbm3` with 1,816,438 and `cdlRenderSpan` with 870,470. `fbm3` calls `noise3` once per octave, and `noise3` calls `q_floor` three times, `q_fract` three times (each of which calls `q_floor` again) and `gnoise` eight times: 14 calls per `noise3`, against 8 in the `kernel` image, which inlines `q_floor` and `q_fract` (f32 backend diary step 8). Each call pays the convention of section 5.4: argument moves, `jal`, result move, and in the callee a frame and saved registers. The constants `3.0` and `2.0` in `noise3`'s smoothing polynomial, and `1.0` and `±2.1e9` in `q_floor`, are each rebuilt at every use in two or three instructions. And O2 makes 21 of Candela's functions *slower* than r0, the largest `fbm3` (+37,458 instructions), because the copies that `licm` and `lvn` introduce into loops cost a move each (f32 backend diary step 9).
310:| copies between locals that never hold different values at once | Blend `t7 = i`, sort `t5/t6/t8 = j`, licm/lvn copies in Candela | `coalesce` option (§13) |
312:| calls to small functions and to functions with one call site | `noise3`: 14 calls against 8; 55 single-site functions in B-01 | `inline` pass (§15) |
318:The milestone has five pieces: two passes on `typed.blocks` (`rotate` and `inline`) and three backend options (`layout: 'loops'`, `coalesce` and `homes`). Each can be turned on alone, measured alone and reviewed alone. The three backend options share one notion of where a function spends its time, the block frequencies of section 11, which the layout uses to order blocks and which the other two use to decide whether they pay.
